ting all three sources; source A explicitly retained \"Apply loop tiling (blocking) in the submatrix sum search phase with block size 64\". So to honor integration, we should include that transformation.\n\nThus our plan: combine fast input + on-the-fly prefix sum (A), OpenMP parallel for reduction over rows schedule(static) (B/C), NEON intrinsics inside each row's column loop (B), and loop tiling with block size 64 inside the column loop (A). Also include restrict pointers? Source A added \"restrict pointers\" but that is C99 feature; in C++ we can use __restrict__ or just rely on compiler. We'll add __restrict__ to pointer declarations for performance.\n\nWe must ensure no compilation errors: need to include <cstdint> for int64_t, and conditionally include arm_neon.h.\n\nAlso note that source A used #pragma ivdep; we can keep it inside the inner loop if desired but not necessary.\n\nNow write the code. We'll produce a single file src/solution.cpp with all transformations integrated.\n\nWe need to output JSON with hypothesis, transformations list (describing what we did), sources_kept array containing the three IDs, files array with path and content, notes.\n\nHypothesis: \"Integrating fast integer input using fread buffer (source A), flattened integral image constructed on-the-fly during input reading (A), OpenMP parallel for reduction(max) over rows with schedule(static) (B,C), ARM NEON SIMD vectorization of submatrix sum computation processing two columns per iteration (B), and loop tiling in the column search phase with block size 64 to improve cache locality (A) yields a correct, high-performance solution on the target ARM64 platform.\"\n\nTransformations list: we can enumerate each retained transformation from sources. But careful: The transformations field should be an array of strings describing what we did. We'll list:\n- \"Replace scanf/iostream input with custom fast integer reader using fread buffer\"\n- \"Eliminate separate matrix storage by building integral image on-the-fly during input reading\"\n- \"Add restrict pointers and ivdep pragmas for auto-vectorization of inner loops\" (if we include them)\n- \"Apply loop tiling (blocking) in the submatrix sum search phase with block size 64\"\n- \"Replace sliding-window with integral-image (prefix-sum) algorithm\"\n- \"Flatten prefix-sum storage into a single contiguous array for SIMD-friendly memory access\"\n- \"Introduce explicit ARM NEON intrinsics to compute two submatrix sums per iteration using int64x2_t vectors\"\n- \"Add OpenMP parallel for reduction(max:mxSum) over rows with static scheduling\"\n- \"Guard NEON code with #ifdef __ARM_NEON and provide scalar fallback\"\n\nBut note that some transformations are redundant (e.g., integral-image algorithm is already covered by A's on-the-fly construction). We'll list all distinct ones.\n\nSources kept: [\"fef0c26a621d4971\", \"5060afd492ee446e\", \"6d295b5f520242c0\"]\n\nNow write the code. Let's produce content for src/solution.cpp.\n\nWe need to be careful about variable types and ensure no overflow in row_sum (use int64_t). Also, the prefix sum array should be zero-initialized; we can use std::vector<int64_t> ps(stride * stride, 0); but that initializes all elements. However, during construction we only set values for i>=1,j>=1; others remain zero which is correct.\n\nIn NEON code, we need to load two int64_t values at positions j+s and j from base_is and base_i arrays. The indices are within bounds because j <= n-s, so j+s <= n, and stride = n+1, so index (i+s)*stride + j+s is valid.\n\nWe'll use vld1q_s64 with pointers to the two consecutive elements: &base_is[j+s] loads base_is[j+s] and base_is[j+s+1]. But we need to ensure that when j = n-s-1, j+s+1 = n? Actually if s=3, n=5, then n-s=2. For j=0, j+s=3; for j=1 (step 2), j=1 => j+s=4; but we need two elements: indices 4 and 5? But index 5 is out of bounds because stride =6, max column index is 5? Actually prefix sum array has size (n+1)*(n+1) with indices from 0 to n inclusive. So for i+s row, the valid columns are 0..n. For j=1 and s=3, j+s=4, so we load base_is[4] and base_is[5]; index 5 is within bounds (since stride =6). But when j=n-s-1? Let's compute: n=5,s=3 => n-s=2. The last starting column is j=2. For NEON step of 2, we process j=0 and then j=2? Actually if we loop for(j=0; j<=n-s; j+=2), then j values are 0,2. At j=2, j+s=5, so we load base_is[5] and base_is[6]; but index 6 is out of bounds because stride =6 => max column index is 5 (since columns go from 0 to n inclusive? Actually prefix sum has indices 0..n for each row; that's n+1 elements per row. So valid indices are 0..n. For n=5, indices 0..5. Index 6 is invalid. So we must ensure that when j = n-s (the last starting column), we cannot load two columns because the second would be out of bounds. Therefore, in NEON loop, we should only go up to j <= n-s - 1? Actually we need to process pairs where both j and j+1 are valid start indices. So condition: for(j=0; j+1 <= n-s; j+=2). Then handle the last column separately if (n-s) is odd.\n\nSource B's code used \"for (j = 0; j + 1 <= jmax; j += 2)\" where jmax = n - s. That is correct: they process pairs while j+1 <= jmax, meaning both j and j+1 are valid start columns. Then after loop, handle remaining column if any.\n\nWe'll adopt that.\n\nAlso note: In NEON code, we need to compute sum for each pair and update mxSum with max of the two lane values. We can use vgetq_lane_s64 or store to array and compare. Source B used:\nint64_t s0 = vgetq_lane_s64(sum, 0);\nint64_t s1 = vgetq_lane_s64(sum, 1);\nif (s0 > mxSum) mxSum = s0;\nif (s1 > mxSum) mxSum = s1;\n\nThat is fine.\n\nNow write the complete code. We'll include <cstdint>, <arm_neon.h> conditionally, and use OpenMP pragma.\n\nWe also need to ensure that we don't have any syntax errors like missing semicolons or braces.\n\nLet's produce the content:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <vector>\n#include <cstdint>\n#ifdef __ARM_NEON\n#include <arm_neon.h>\n#endif\n\n// Fast integer reader using fread buffer (from source A)\nstatic int read_int() {\n    static char buf[1 << 20];\n    static size_t pos = 0, len = 0;\n    while (pos >= len) {\n        len = fread(buf, 1, sizeof(buf), stdin);\n        if (!len) return 0;\n        pos = 0;\n    }\n    while (buf[pos] < '0' && buf[pos] != '-') {\n        ++pos;\n        if (pos >= len) {\n            len = fread(buf, 1, sizeof(buf), stdin);\n            if (!len) return 0;\n            pos = 0;\n        }\n    }\n    bool neg = false;\n    if (buf[pos] == '-') { neg = true; ++pos; }\n    int x = 0;\n    while (true) {\n        if (pos >= len) {\n            size_t nread = fread(buf, 1, sizeof(buf), stdin);\n            if (!nread) break;\n            pos = 0;\n            len = nread;\n        }\n        char c = buf[pos];\n        if (c < '0' || c > '9') break;\n        x = x * 10 + (c - '0');\n        ++pos;\n    }\n    return neg ? -x : x;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int n = read_int();\n    int s = read_int();\n\n    const int stride = n + 1;\n    std::vector<int64_t> ps(stride * stride, 0);\n    int64_t* __restrict__ p = ps.data();\n\n    // Build integral image on-the-fly during input reading (source A)\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n        int64_t row_sum = 0;\n        int base_cur = (i + 1) * stride;\n        int base_prev = i * stride;\n        #pragma ivdep\n        for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n            int val = read_int();\n            row_sum += val;\n            p[base_cur + j + 1] = p[base_prev + j + 1] + row_sum;\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Search for maximum SxS submatrix sum with OpenMP, NEON, and loop tiling\n    int64_t mxSum = -(1LL << 60);\n    const int limit_i = n - s;      // inclusive upper bound for starting rows\n    const int limit_j = n - s;      // inclusive upper bound for starting columns\n    const int BLOCK = 32;           // tiling block size (source A used 64, but we use 32 to balance)\n\n    #pragma omp parallel for reduction(max:mxSum) schedule(static)\n    for (int i = 0; i <= limit_i; ++i) {\n        const int64_t* __restrict__ base_i   = p + i * stride;\n        const int64_t* __restrict__ base_is  = p + (i + s) * stride;\n\n        // Loop tiling over columns\n        for (int jj = 0; jj <= limit_j; jj += BLOCK) {\n            int j_end = jj + BLOCK;\n            if (j_end > limit_j + 1) j_end = limit_j + 1; // inclusive upper bound\n\n#ifdef __ARM_NEON\n            int j = jj;\n            for (; j + 1 < j_end; j += 2) {\n                const int64x2_t v_a = vld1q_s64(&base_is[j + s]);\n                const int64x2_t v_b = vld1q_s64(&base_i [j + s]);\n                const int64x2_t v_c = vld1q_s64(&base_is[j]);\n                const int64x2_t v_d = vld1q_s64(&base_i [j]);\n\n                int64x2_t sum = vsubq_s64(v_a, v_b);\n                sum = vsubq_s64(sum, v_c);\n                sum = vaddq_s64(sum, v_d);\n\n                const int64_t s0 = vgetq_lane_s64(sum, 0);\n                const int64_t s1 = vgetq_lane_s64(sum, 1);\n                if (s0 > mxSum) mxSum = s0;\n                if (s1 > mxSum) mxSum = s1;\n            }\n            // Handle remaining column in this block\n            for (; j < j_end; ++j) {\n#else\n            for (int j = jj; j < j_end; ++j) {\n#endif\n                const int64_t sum_val = base_is[j + s] - base_i[j + s]\n                                      - base_is[j]     + base_i[j];\n                if (sum_val > mxSum) mxSum = sum_val;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    printf(\"%lld\\n\", static_cast<long long>(mxSum));\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nWe used BLOCK=32 instead of 64 to be conservative; but we can keep 64 as in source A. However, with NEON processing two columns at a time, block size should be multiple of 2 for best alignment? Not necessary. We'll use 64.\n\nAlso note: In the tiling loop, j_end is set to min(jj+BLOCK, limit_j+1) because we want inclusive upper bound (j <= limit_j). So condition j < j_end works correctly.\n\nWe added restrict pointers on p and base_i/base_is. Also ivdep pragma in input reading loop.\n\nPotential issue: The NEON code uses vld1q_s64 which expects 16-byte aligned pointer for best performance, but we are loading from potentially unaligned addresses (since j+s may not be multiple of 2). ARM allows unaligned loads; it's fine. We could add __builtin_assume_aligned if needed.\n\nAlso note: In the NEON loop, we used base_is[j + s] and base_i[j + s]; these are int64_t pointers, so indexing works correctly.\n\nNow check for correctness of indices: For a given i,j, sum = ps[(i+s)*stride + j+s] - ps[i*stride + j+s] - ps[(i+s)*stride + j] + ps[i*stride + j]. Our base_is points to row (i+s), base_i points to row i. So base_is[j+s] = ps[(i+s)*stride + j+s], etc. Good.\n\nNow ensure that the parallel reduction works: mxSum is shared and reduced with max. We initialized it to a very negative number (-(1LL<<60) which is about -1e18, safe). The problem guarantees result fits 32-bit signed int, so this initial value will be replaced by first sum.\n\nWe must include <cstdint> for int64_t. Also note that we used __restrict__ which is GCC extension; it's fine on ARM with GCC.\n\nNow produce the JSON output.\n\nHypothesis: \"Integrating fast integer input using fread buffer (source A), flattened integral image constructed on-the-fly during input reading (A), OpenMP parallel for reduction(max) over rows with schedule(static) (B,C), ARM NEON SIMD vectorization of submatrix sum computation processing two columns per iteration (B), and loop tiling in the column search phase with block size 64 to improve cache locality (A) yields a correct, high-performance solution on the target ARM64 platform.\"\n\nTransformations: list all retained transformations from sources. We'll include:\n- \"Replace scanf/iostream input with custom fast integer reader using fread buffer\"\n- \"Eliminate separate matrix storage by building integral image on-the-fly during input reading\"\n- \"Add restrict pointers and ivdep pragmas for auto-vectorization of inner loops\"\n- \"Apply loop tiling (blocking) in the submatrix sum search phase with block size 64\"\n- \"Replace sliding-window with integral-image (prefix-sum) algorithm\"\n- \"Flatten prefix-sum storage into a single contiguous array for SIMD-friendly memory access\"\n- \"Introduce explicit ARM NEON intrinsics to compute two submatrix sums per iteration using int64x2_t vectors\"\n- \"Add OpenMP parallel for reduction(max:mxSum) over rows with static scheduling\"\n- \"Guard NEON code with #ifdef __ARM_NEON and provide scalar fallback\"\n\nBut note that some transformations are from multiple sources; we can list them once.\n\nSources kept: [\"fef0c26a621d4971\", \"5060afd492ee446e\", \"6d295b5f520242c0\"]\n\nFiles: [{\"path\": \"src/solution.cpp\", \"content\": \"<the code>\"}]\n\nNotes: \"This integration combines the fast input and on-the-fly integral image construction from source A, OpenMP row-parallelism with NEON SIMD vectorization from source B (including scalar fallback), loop tiling for cache efficiency from source A, and uses contiguous flattened storage. The collapse(2) approach from source C was not used because it conflicts with manual NEON vectorization; instead we adopt the row-parallel reduction which allows efficient SIMD usage.\"\n\nNow produce JSON."
